Why more frequent data is worth having
• Decisions on current data: on a monthly cycle, a dashboard figure can be a month old or more by the time someone reads it. A weekly cycle brings that under a week; a daily cycle, to about a day.
• Problems caught while they're easy to fix: each submission is validated as it arrives, so a contributor hears about a missing field the same day, not after the month closes.
• No month-end crunch: processing and exception review are spread across the month instead of landing in one push.
• Trends seen as they develop: leadership can spot a shift mid-month and act before the cycle closes.
• Smaller, safer batches: a bad file affects one day's rows and is easy to rerun. Dataverse also holds less data at any moment, which keeps it further under its included storage.
• Gains at any cadence: resubmissions update rows instead of duplicating them, and every submission is logged with its sender, time and outcome, so a disputed number traces back to its source file.
A faster cadence gives fresher data only if contributors send data more often. If they keep a monthly schedule, the pipeline still processes each file the day it arrives, removing the wait for staff to get to it.
Security and compliance: non-CUI data, kept inside the existing government tenant
The program's data isn't CUI, so the pipeline fits either government cloud. It runs entirely inside the tenant the program already uses and adds no new cloud service, vendor or identity system.
• Authorization: Power Apps in GCC meets federal cloud requirements including FedRAMP High and DoD DISA IL2. In GCC High, Microsoft designed it to meet DISA SRG IL4, and DISA has granted a Provisional Authority to Operate (Microsoft Learn). Microsoft lists Dataverse as generally available at FedRAMP High and DoD IL4 in Azure Government (Microsoft Learn).
• Identity: GCC uses public Microsoft Entra ID, and GCC High requires Microsoft Entra Government (Microsoft Learn). Either way, existing accounts, multifactor rules and conditional access apply unchanged.
• Least privilege: Dataverse security roles limit staged rows to the pipeline account and the two named reviewers.
• Audit trail: Dataverse auditing logs user access and changes made to records (Microsoft Learn).
• Less data held: staged rows are deleted once published, so Dataverse never becomes a second long-term copy of program data.
• Connector control: admins choose which connectors may be used through data policies in the Power Platform admin center. In GCC High, new connectors are also disabled by default until an admin allows them (Microsoft Learn).
Before the pilot, we will confirm with the program's ISSM whether enabling a Dataverse environment requires an update to the system security plan.
